  "explanation": {
    "short": "Hindi has two kinds of t and two kinds of d. For त and द the tongue touches the back of the top teeth. For ट and ड the tongue tip curls up and back and taps the roof of the mouth. तीन (three) and टीन (a tin) are different words; so are दाल (lentils) and डाल (a branch).",
    "more": "English t and d are in between, and Hindi ears usually hear them as the curled ones, ट ड. In the letters under the words, a dot under the letter marks the curled ones: ṭ ḍ. Try saying तीन, then टीन."
  },
